DAY 45 - PART A: THEORY
Cloud Registries and Cloud Load Balancers
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. CLOUD CONTAINER REGISTRIES
-----------------------------
  AWS ECR, Azure ACR, Google Artifact Registry.
Why not Docker Hub? Private by default, close to your nodes (fast,
no rate limits), IAM-based access (nodes pull with their IAM role, no
imagePullSecrets), vulnerability scanning, lifecycle policies.

ECR image name:
  <account-id>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com/<repo>:<tag>
Login for docker/podman uses a 12-hour token:
  aws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <registry>
EKS nodes' IAM role has AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly, so pulls
just work.


2. Service type: LoadBalancer IN THE CLOUD
------------------------------------------
The cloud controller (or AWS Load Balancer Controller) watches
LoadBalancer Services and creates a real LB:

  Internet -> cloud LB (public subnets) -> NodePort on nodes
           -> kube-proxy -> pods
  or, with "IP target" mode -> directly to pod IPs (VPC CNI)

On EKS:
  Default (no extra controller)    Classic LB / NLB via the built-in
                                   cloud provider (legacy)
  AWS Load Balancer Controller     NLB for Services, ALB for Ingress;
                                   the recommended way
Annotations choose details, e.g.
  service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-scheme: internet-facing
  service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: external   (LB controller)


3. INGRESS IN THE CLOUD
-----------------------
Option A: one LoadBalancer Service in front of an in-cluster ingress
          controller (Traefik, NGINX ...) -> 1 cloud LB for all apps.
Option B: AWS LB Controller turns each Ingress (class "alb") into an
          Application Load Balancer; group several Ingresses on one
          ALB with the group.name annotation.
GKE: Ingress -> Google HTTP(S) LB by default. AKS: Application
Gateway for Containers or an in-cluster controller.


4. DNS AND TLS IN THE CLOUD
---------------------------
external-dns can create Route 53/Azure DNS/Cloud DNS records from
Services/Ingresses. TLS: cert-manager (Week 8) or cloud certificates
(AWS ACM on the ALB/NLB).


5. COST NOTE
------------
Every LoadBalancer Service = one cloud LB billed hourly + traffic.
Deleting the cluster WITHOUT deleting LB Services first can leave
orphaned load balancers (and block VPC deletion). Always delete
LoadBalancer Services and Ingresses before the cluster.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Push images to the cloud registry; nodes pull with IAM.
- LoadBalancer Service -> real cloud LB; Ingress -> ALB (with the
  controller) or LB + in-cluster controller.
- Delete LBs before deleting the cluster.
